WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.292 --> 00:00:06.294
So there's this very famous song.

00:00:06.336 --> 00:00:08.129
I'm sure you're all familiar with it.

00:00:08.171 --> 00:00:10.757
It’s called “My Way” by Frank Sinatra.

00:00:11.591 --> 00:00:13.385
Now I love it,

00:00:13.426 --> 00:00:17.305
but there's one line in that song
that has always bothered me.

00:00:18.181 --> 00:00:19.391
It goes like this.

00:00:20.809 --> 00:00:22.185
(Sings) “Regrets,

00:00:22.227 --> 00:00:24.688
I've had a few.

00:00:25.021 --> 00:00:29.609
But then again, too few to mention."

00:00:30.902 --> 00:00:32.112
Regrets.

00:00:32.821 --> 00:00:33.989
I've had a few.

00:00:35.156 --> 00:00:38.493
But then again, too few to mention?

00:00:38.952 --> 00:00:40.245
Now hang on a second.

00:00:40.745 --> 00:00:45.208
I am pretty certain that Frank Sinatra
had more than a few regrets

00:00:45.208 --> 00:00:46.376
(Laughter)

00:00:46.418 --> 00:00:49.337
Including hanging out with shady mafiosos.

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:50.589
So there's that.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:56.094
But that song, and society in general,

00:00:56.136 --> 00:01:00.223
encourages us to minimize regret,

00:01:00.265 --> 00:01:03.643
as if they really don't have
an impact on our lives.

00:01:04.311 --> 00:01:06.062
But that's just not true.

00:01:06.479 --> 00:01:07.814
For many of us,

00:01:07.814 --> 00:01:10.734
regrets are at the core of our identity

00:01:10.775 --> 00:01:13.612
and have a huge impact on our work,

00:01:13.612 --> 00:01:16.573
our relationships
and how we live our lives.

00:01:18.116 --> 00:01:20.452
I certainly have a few.

00:01:20.493 --> 00:01:22.662
Some aren't very important.

00:01:23.079 --> 00:01:25.498
I regret eating a box of donuts

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:28.126
right before my annual physical last year.

00:01:28.126 --> 00:01:29.294
(Laughter)

00:01:30.045 --> 00:01:32.213
Some are a little bit more serious.

00:01:32.213 --> 00:01:37.302
I regret not spending more time
with my son after my divorce.

00:01:39.012 --> 00:01:42.849
But I have one deep regret

00:01:42.849 --> 00:01:47.395
that goes right to the heart
of what I'm talking about today.

00:01:49.981 --> 00:01:52.150
You see,

00:01:52.192 --> 00:01:53.985
I'm an accidental killer.

00:01:55.445 --> 00:01:57.906
When I was a young man,

00:01:57.906 --> 00:02:00.784
I was driving home late one night

00:02:00.784 --> 00:02:03.536
with my high school sweetheart, Michelle.

00:02:04.496 --> 00:02:09.334
And I was paying more attention to her
than I was to my driving.

00:02:10.835 --> 00:02:13.129
And I went off the road,

00:02:13.129 --> 00:02:14.923
and I hit a telephone pole.

00:02:16.466 --> 00:02:17.717
And she died.

00:02:20.011 --> 00:02:23.014
For decades after that,

00:02:23.014 --> 00:02:26.351
there were times where
I was completely overwhelmed

00:02:26.351 --> 00:02:30.480
and brought to my knees
by incredible guilt,

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.651
paralyzing shame and sorrow and regret.

00:02:35.527 --> 00:02:37.028
A lot of self-loathing.

00:02:37.779 --> 00:02:39.823
Not very much self-respect.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:44.494
And a desperate need
to do whatever I could

00:02:44.536 --> 00:02:47.580
to pay back the life that I took.

00:02:49.582 --> 00:02:53.545
And for many years, I thought
I was the only one in this situation.

00:02:54.879 --> 00:02:56.131
I was wrong.

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:58.925
Every single year,

00:02:58.967 --> 00:03:02.220
people in the US
accidentally kill someone.

00:03:02.762 --> 00:03:07.058
And many more are seriously injured
or permanently disabled

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:11.354
because someone made
an unintentional mistake,

00:03:11.396 --> 00:03:13.356
just like me.

00:03:14.941 --> 00:03:19.446
We all belong to a club
that we never wanted to join.

00:03:20.947 --> 00:03:24.743
And recently I found out
that that club has a name.

00:03:25.493 --> 00:03:28.079
It's called the Hyacinth Fellowship.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:30.957
And last fall, I had the opportunity

00:03:30.957 --> 00:03:35.044
to attend their first annual
in-person gathering in Detroit.

00:03:35.879 --> 00:03:39.174
All perfectly normal, reasonable,

00:03:39.215 --> 00:03:42.844
caring people from all walks of life

00:03:42.886 --> 00:03:47.390
coming together to try to figure out a way

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:51.978
to move forward and to cope
through their own accidental killings.

00:03:53.396 --> 00:03:56.483
One woman I met there,
let's call her Marie,

00:03:56.483 --> 00:04:00.528
she was just one year out
from having had a motorcyclist

00:04:00.528 --> 00:04:03.490
T-bone, her car, dying instantly.

00:04:04.324 --> 00:04:08.495
And she told us
that she had recently found out

00:04:08.536 --> 00:04:12.332
that two cars had collided
in that exact same spot

00:04:12.373 --> 00:04:14.417
not long after her accident,

00:04:14.417 --> 00:04:16.544
and both the drivers had died.

00:04:17.504 --> 00:04:22.467
And she said her very first thought was,

00:04:22.509 --> 00:04:24.552
"Thank goodness they both died,

00:04:24.594 --> 00:04:27.514
because then no one
has to feel the guilt."

00:04:28.723 --> 00:04:33.019
And we all nodded because
we knew exactly how she felt.

00:04:34.562 --> 00:04:38.316
And one of the realizations I took away
with me from that gathering

00:04:38.358 --> 00:04:41.528
is how ill-equipped we are as a society

00:04:41.528 --> 00:04:43.404
to deal with regret.

00:04:44.489 --> 00:04:49.035
Now accidentally killing
someone is very, very serious.

00:04:49.994 --> 00:04:52.705
But none of us are immune to regret.

00:04:53.414 --> 00:04:54.916
Maybe, as I've been talking,

00:04:54.958 --> 00:04:57.502
some of you here have been
thinking about something

00:04:57.544 --> 00:04:59.462
that happened in your life.

00:04:59.504 --> 00:05:03.842
Some unintentional harm you caused
someone that you truly regret,

00:05:03.842 --> 00:05:07.178
that you wish you could
take back and fix somehow.

00:05:08.221 --> 00:05:10.098
The thing of it is,

00:05:10.139 --> 00:05:12.976
most of us don't talk about our regrets

00:05:13.017 --> 00:05:14.936
until maybe we're on our deathbed,

00:05:14.978 --> 00:05:18.106
trying to own our mistakes
and make amends.

00:05:18.773 --> 00:05:20.608
But by then it's usually too late.

00:05:21.526 --> 00:05:24.070
Or if we do talk about them,

00:05:24.112 --> 00:05:26.781
we often get some really bad advice.

00:05:28.116 --> 00:05:34.205
I remember one woman coming up
to me at the funeral and saying,

00:05:34.205 --> 00:05:35.540
"Don't blame yourself.

00:05:35.582 --> 00:05:37.125
This isn't your fault.

00:05:37.125 --> 00:05:39.085
God wanted her in heaven."

00:05:40.879 --> 00:05:44.257
Now I know she was trying
to make me feel better,

00:05:44.299 --> 00:05:46.551
and I know she truly believed that.

00:05:47.176 --> 00:05:49.345
But at that moment, all I could think was,

00:05:49.387 --> 00:05:51.055
"Are you kidding me?"

00:05:52.724 --> 00:05:55.101
But the really troubling piece of advice

00:05:55.143 --> 00:06:00.064
that I got over and over and over again

00:06:00.064 --> 00:06:03.109
was that I needed to forgive myself.

00:06:04.485 --> 00:06:05.653
Huh.

00:06:06.279 --> 00:06:07.614
Forgive myself.

00:06:08.948 --> 00:06:11.451
That is the last thing I want to do.

00:06:12.452 --> 00:06:14.412
You see, to me,

00:06:14.412 --> 00:06:19.000
forgiving myself is somehow
making an excuse for my failure

00:06:19.042 --> 00:06:21.878
or deliberately letting
myself off the hook

00:06:21.878 --> 00:06:24.464
so I could feel better about me.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:28.843
No, I was wholly responsible
for what happened that night.

00:06:28.885 --> 00:06:30.386
And I always will be.

00:06:31.429 --> 00:06:34.057
So forgiving myself is off the table.

00:06:35.058 --> 00:06:40.480
But what I always needed to know was
how do I move forward in a positive way?

00:06:41.898 --> 00:06:46.444
One thing I discovered
is you can regret something

00:06:46.444 --> 00:06:48.780
and hold yourself accountable for it,

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:51.074
but that doesn't mean
you have to punish yourself

00:06:51.115 --> 00:06:52.533
for the rest of your life.

00:06:54.535 --> 00:06:57.372
You could learn from your regrets.

00:06:57.372 --> 00:07:01.417
You could try to transform them
or try to do something good with them.

00:07:02.418 --> 00:07:05.797
You know, one of the best
pieces of advice I ever got

00:07:05.838 --> 00:07:09.425
came from a friend of mine
in the form of two questions,

00:07:09.467 --> 00:07:12.261
and he asked me this right
after the accident.

00:07:12.303 --> 00:07:15.932
He said, "So, dude, this happened to you.

00:07:16.724 --> 00:07:18.267
What have you learned?

00:07:18.309 --> 00:07:20.186
And what are you going to do about it?”

00:07:21.604 --> 00:07:23.398
Pretty harsh, right?

00:07:24.399 --> 00:07:26.067
Still, he had a point.

00:07:26.985 --> 00:07:32.115
But I was too overcome
with sorrow and regret and shame

00:07:32.115 --> 00:07:34.200
to really think it through.

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:38.162
And that's the thing about regrets.

00:07:38.705 --> 00:07:40.873
We can try to ignore them,

00:07:40.873 --> 00:07:43.042
we can try to dismiss them,

00:07:43.084 --> 00:07:47.505
we can even let them overwhelm
us and undermine our relationships

00:07:47.547 --> 00:07:49.924
and our work for the rest of our lives.

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:53.594
Or we can choose a different path forward.

00:07:54.512 --> 00:07:55.722
To own our mistakes,

00:07:55.763 --> 00:07:59.642
to do whatever we can
to help those we harmed

00:07:59.684 --> 00:08:01.352
and make amends any way we can,

00:08:01.394 --> 00:08:05.648
and somehow try to make a positive
difference in the world.

00:08:07.108 --> 00:08:09.819
So the question then becomes, how?

00:08:11.112 --> 00:08:15.116
Well, for many years,
I tried to do "good works"

00:08:15.158 --> 00:08:18.995
in my work as a professional
theater artist and in my writing

00:08:19.037 --> 00:08:22.373
and in my training programs
on leadership and on respect.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:27.378
But looking back on it now,

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:31.549
I think that all that
good work I was doing

00:08:31.549 --> 00:08:34.469
was unconsciously disingenuous.

00:08:35.595 --> 00:08:39.724
You see, as good as they were,
those things I was doing,

00:08:39.766 --> 00:08:43.978
I think I was just doing them
to make myself feel better about me

00:08:43.978 --> 00:08:46.773
and to convince other people
that I'm a good guy.

00:08:47.648 --> 00:08:50.485
Which I am, just ask
my three-month-old grandson.

00:08:50.526 --> 00:08:52.612
(Laughter)

00:08:53.571 --> 00:08:59.619
Eventually, after doing some research
on something called “moral injury,”

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:02.330
which is where we intentionally

00:09:02.371 --> 00:09:07.085
or unintentionally break
our own personal moral code,

00:09:07.126 --> 00:09:09.837
I realized that if I could make

00:09:09.837 --> 00:09:12.381
a powerful, positive
difference in the world

00:09:12.423 --> 00:09:17.220
in the way that Michelle
would have if she had lived

00:09:17.261 --> 00:09:20.681
or would have told me to do, if she could,

00:09:20.723 --> 00:09:25.144
then all the good I was doing
would be wholly about her

00:09:25.144 --> 00:09:27.563
and not about assuaging my guilt.

00:09:28.689 --> 00:09:31.150
So what I did was, seven years ago,

00:09:31.150 --> 00:09:35.404
I started an art scholarship
in her name at our high school.

00:09:36.072 --> 00:09:38.991
She wanted to be a professional artist,

00:09:38.991 --> 00:09:43.538
and it feels like that's exactly
what she would have wanted me to do.

00:09:53.631 --> 00:09:56.134
And you know what I regret now?

00:09:59.387 --> 00:10:02.431
Is not coming to that realization sooner.

00:10:05.476 --> 00:10:10.940
Don't try to make the world better
to make yourself feel better about you.

00:10:10.982 --> 00:10:14.902
Change the world in the way
that those you hurt would have done

00:10:14.902 --> 00:10:18.322
or would have told you
to do if they could.

00:10:19.866 --> 00:10:23.494
The woman from the Hyacinth
Fellowship I told you about,

00:10:23.494 --> 00:10:28.583
well, she is now actively
researching practical ways

00:10:28.624 --> 00:10:32.503
to make our roadways safer
for motorcyclists.

00:10:33.546 --> 00:10:37.925
And many in the group have become
lay pastors and counselors and mentors

00:10:37.967 --> 00:10:40.928
to those who are still working
their way through

00:10:40.928 --> 00:10:43.389
their own accidental killings.

00:10:45.474 --> 00:10:46.726
Regrets.

00:10:47.435 --> 00:10:49.312
We all have a few, right?

00:10:50.021 --> 00:10:51.397
And that's OK.

00:10:53.232 --> 00:10:55.026
You can't change your past.

00:10:56.736 --> 00:11:00.656
But you can use it to correct the future.

00:11:02.366 --> 00:11:03.534
Thank you.

00:11:03.576 --> 00:11:07.371
(Cheers and applause)

